The two addresses carry two different licences, and that is not a mistake in
either of them: a licence attaches to a work, not to a project. The repository
licenses the training and inference code; the checkpoint published on Hugging
Face is a separate artefact with its own declared terms. The same authors are
free to grant each of them differently, and here they did.

Which of the two reaches us is settled by what we ship: not one line of their
code. The forward pass in internal/ced is written from scratch in Go — that is
what the parity against ced.cpp exists to demonstrate — so the only thing of
theirs inside the binary is the file below. Had their implementation been
vendored, GPL-3.0 would have followed it in.

The repository says nothing separate about the checkpoints, so anyone who
checks that address alone will find GPL-3.0 and conclude this notice is wrong.
It is not: the grant we rely on is the one on the model card, and the weights
in this project came from there.

The file this project ships is those weights converted to GGUF and quantised to
Q8_0. It is not a modification of the model: the architecture, the training and
the parameters are unchanged, and the conversion only rewrites how the numbers
are stored.

